Spec: Project/Architecture

Purpose:
  Compact, token-efficient description of the project's technical shape,
  using the symbol vocabulary defined in protocol/symbols.sdd for state
  only — these P{} / M{} blocks are a separate, project-level shorthand
  for stack/module facts, not stage state.

P {
  n:  EduNexus
  a:  MM        # MM = Modular Monolith
  be: GO        # GO = Golang
  db: PG        # PG = PostgreSQL
  fe: RX        # RX = React
  md: RN        # RN = React Native
  dp: DK        # DK = Docker
}

ModuleIsolation:
  Purpose:
    Keep the modular monolith extraction-ready (microservice-ready) by
    enforcing boundaries between modules before they become entangled.

  ExtractCheck verifies, per module boundary:
    DB isolation
    dependency isolation
    domain isolation
    API contract
    infrastructure isolation
    shared state
    circular dependency

  Example violation:
    orders -> users DB      # cross-module-db, FORBIDDEN

  On violation:
    result: -
    reason: cross-module-db
    chain returns: EXTRACT-CHECK -> MODULE -> DESIGN -> CODE
    (i.e. the chain does not just report the error — it routes back to the
    stage responsible for fixing it; see protocol/rules.sdd)

Must:
  Every module declared in project/modules.sdd MUST pass ExtractCheck
  before its chain is allowed to reach QA.

Forbids:
  Direct cross-module database access.
  Circular module dependencies.

LayerArchitecture (prompt/new/16.md):
  # ModuleIsolation above answers "can module X touch module Y's DB?".
  # This section answers a different question: "inside one module, which
  # layer is allowed to call which?" — a structural dependency graph, not
  # a work-order. It MUST NOT be confused with a module's execution chain
  # (AN>AR>DB>BE>API>FE>MD>QA>DO>VR, see protocol/stages.sdd) — that is
  # "what order do I implement in", this is "what may call what at
  # runtime". Conflating the two was STEP 4's original mistake and is why
  # project/<module>/flow.sdd was retracted (see Note at the bottom).

  DefaultAllowed:
    FE  -> API
    MD  -> API
    BE  -> DB
    BE  -> ExternalGateway   # e.g. PaymentGateway, see project/integrations.sdd
    QA  -> API
    QA  -> Application

  DefaultForbidden:
    FE  -> DB
    MD  -> DB
    FE  -> Repository        # any module's persistence layer
    MD  -> Repository

  # Module-local overrides/extensions of this default (e.g. Payment's own
  # allowed/forbidden edges) live in project/<module>/architecture.sdd —
  # not yet built (planned STEP 5, prompt/new/16.md's own roadmap). Do not
  # anticipate that file's content here (R2/R3 — don't duplicate module
  # knowledge at the global level ahead of it actually being resolved).

  State:
    + = compliant
    ! = violation
    ? = architecture decision required
    ~ = architecture change in progress

  ViolationVsDecision:
    # AI MUST NOT silently resolve which of these two it's looking at.
    # Same observed edge (e.g. FE -> DB), two different causes:
    #   A) developer/AI mistake            -> state: !  (Architecture Violation)
    #   B) a deliberate new architecture   -> state: ?  (Architecture Decision
    #      decision was made, this file          Required — human decision,
    #      just hasn't been updated yet          per PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision,
    #                                            "architecture changes")
    # AI raises `?` and stops; it does not silently mark `!` and "fix" the
    # edge, and it does not silently accept the edge as the new normal.

Note (prompt/new/16.md, retracts prompt/new/15.md's STEP 4):
  The source chat caught its own mistake: project/<module>/flow.sdd, built
  in the previous step as "AN > AR > DB > BE > API > FE > MD > QA > DO >
  VR", conflated two unrelated things — architecture (what may call what)
  and execution order (what gets implemented in what order). It has been
  removed (see project/payment/ — flow.sdd deleted).

  Corrected 3-layer split going forward:
    Architecture  -> what is allowed to depend on what      (this file /
                      project/<module>/architecture.sdd, STEP 5)
    Chain         -> what happens next, work order           (.sdd/chains/
                      *.chain, STEP 8 — NOT module/flow.sdd)
    Skill Chain   -> how a single stage's work gets done      (STEP 9)

  This file (project/architecture.sdd) is the new STEP 4 target: the
  global architecture model + violation rules. Remaining roadmap per
  prompt/new/16.md: STEP 5 module architecture, STEP 6 DB model, STEP 7
  API model, STEP 8 execution chains, STEP 9 skill chains — read one at a
  time, only on "next" (see sddra-next-gated-sdd-build).
